So that's where we started.

539
00:49:52,480 --> 00:50:03,760
And then slowly but surely, we've been expanding that out to include the doctors of the church, the church fathers, and then even some bishop's conferences, meditative, meditative teaching, homilies of the pope's general audiences.

540
00:50:03,840 --> 00:50:16,640
We've been focusing a lot on that lately, mainly because those general audiences and homilies are incredible examples of, you know, basically human beings' ability to destill from, you know, from like very complicated theological texts.

541
00:50:16,720 --> 00:50:19,600
And this pope in fifteen minutes destils it down for people to understand, right?

542
00:50:19,760 --> 00:50:22,160
While at the same time reflecting on why it's relevant to the world.

543
00:50:22,640 --> 00:50:24,640
Like these kinds of examples of destillation for AI.

544
00:50:25,200 --> 00:50:28,560
are really important because people come and they'll bring like a very generalized situation.

545
00:50:28,640 --> 00:50:29,680
Here's what's going on in my life.

546
00:50:29,760 --> 00:50:30,800
What would the church say to me?

547
00:50:31,280 --> 00:50:32,800
And this was a big problem when we started.

548
00:50:32,960 --> 00:50:33,600
It freaked us out.

549
00:50:33,760 --> 00:50:37,040
I mean, at one point I was wondering if we should even be doing this.

550
00:50:37,600 --> 00:50:48,960
And then the only reason we continued, namely, because I didn't think, especially with the early AIs, which weren't that intelligent, I wasn't sure if we should, was I realized if we stopped, people are just going to take those questions to ChatGPT.

551
00:50:49,360 --> 00:50:51,040
And I mean, at least for us, we're trying.

552
00:50:51,280 --> 00:50:52,880
Our objective function is fidelity, right?

553
00:50:53,040 --> 00:50:55,600
So I thought, well, we gotta stay at this, right?

554
00:50:55,760 --> 00:50:59,520
Because it's better than just going back to secular AIs.

555
00:51:00,000 --> 00:51:09,200
This issue of someone sharing a particular situation being able to share that the AI is then able to understand from first principles what the church teaches and then apply it to that particular situation.

556
00:51:10,080 --> 00:51:20,480
The best way, like we found to enable, or I should say the way that we found that was most effective to allow the AI to do that was just by showing innumerable examples of the Pope's and other notable people in the church doing it.

557
00:51:20,800 --> 00:51:23,360
And then the AI was just able to kind of figure it out.

558
00:51:23,600 --> 00:51:34,240
And then, Matthew, the fact that you and the team have ingested, have digitized all this incredible body of work, this is something that's unique that only Magisterium AI has.

559
00:51:34,400 --> 00:51:57,520
Because I think of users who may be may be watching who have questions and they might go to a chat GPT or a Gemini and maybe in some cases they provide the right answer, but often, a lot of times, and this happened to me specifically, we get hallucinations where all of a sudden the AI is generating an answer that is not grounded in a text or in tradition or in anything based on fidelity.

560
00:51:58,640 --> 00:52:03,920
Could you speak on hallucinations and what that is and why that takes place?

561
00:52:04,720 --> 00:52:07,760
Well, first let me just say, just to define what it is.

562
00:52:08,000 --> 00:52:11,280
So hallucination essentially is when an AI makes something up.

563
00:52:12,320 --> 00:52:14,480
And it's coming, I think it's sort of coming from a good place.

564
00:52:14,720 --> 00:52:15,920
I mean, it's very eager to please.

565
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:17,280
It wants to give you an answer, right?

566
00:52:17,520 --> 00:52:22,320
And sometimes it maybe wasn't trained on the data it needs to answer.

567
00:52:22,480 --> 00:52:25,200
And so it just kind of, it just kind of makes it up, right?

568
00:52:25,520 --> 00:52:27,600
So it's not grounded in anything.

569
00:52:28,640 --> 00:52:34,160
And so this is particularly a problem when you're, when you're dealing with the realm of facts, where I mean, it hallucinates a fact.

570
00:52:34,560 --> 00:52:37,360
So it passes something off as a fact, but in fact, it's not actually a fact.

571
00:52:37,920 --> 00:52:38,480
It's not true.

572
00:52:39,200 --> 00:52:41,120
So this is, this is a hallucination.

573
00:52:41,680 --> 00:52:44,560
And obviously, they've been a problem in generative I from the very beginning.

574
00:52:44,640 --> 00:52:46,320
Now, I systems as they got better.

575
00:52:48,960 --> 00:52:51,360
The likelihood of hallucinations is going down over time.

576
00:52:51,600 --> 00:52:57,440
And if you combine it with other techniques like RAG and prompt engineering and tooling, you can bring that probability down.

577
00:52:57,680 --> 00:53:00,960
And that's what we've been able to effectively demonstrate with Mataster MI.

578
00:53:02,480 --> 00:53:08,000
But hallucinations, I think, at least for the foreseeable future, will remain a problem as long as you know, we're using generative AI.

579
00:53:08,240 --> 00:53:15,600
It's just one of the, again, like the way generative AI models, it's just, it's calculating probabilities and it's doing next token prediction, right?

580
00:53:15,680 --> 00:53:18,160
And sometimes those statistical predictions are wrong.

581
00:53:18,800 --> 00:53:26,000
So, and if you compound, compound like next token prediction, one token after another, it tends to be like, we we call it like exponential drift.

582
00:53:26,240 --> 00:53:36,560
Like the longer it talks, the probability that it'll just kind of go off, it'll, it'll, these statistical errors can compound and it can just go off in some weird tangent, which is why this is a very difficult, you know, problem to solve.

583
00:53:36,640 --> 00:53:38,960
But we're getting, I think we're getting closer to cracking it.

584
00:53:39,200 --> 00:53:45,840
And Matthew, when you look at the, uh, the major LLM, you know, the leading, uh, language models, how, how do you score them?

585
00:53:45,920 --> 00:53:47,840
Where, where do they run in terms of fidelity?

586
00:53:48,080 --> 00:54:05,760
Do you, um, well, so in order to, like, score them, so to speak, you have to have what we call in the industry evals, which is basically like, like, question and answer pairs where you have, like a question and you have like the right answer and you need to compile a lot of those.

587
00:54:06,240 --> 00:54:12,880
And so that's something we've been working really hard on is to kind of build the most comprehensive eval set for for fidelity.

588
00:54:13,040 --> 00:54:27,040
Because essentially this is just because we need to do this in order to effectively make matter streaming work, we use state-of-the-art models under the hood, namely because these state-of-the-art models generally every every new model that ships is just better at reasoning.

589
00:54:27,440 --> 00:54:28,720
I say that in quotation marks.

590
00:54:28,880 --> 00:54:29,600
It's just smarter.

591
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:32,320
And it's, therefore, it's also better at instruct following.

592
00:54:32,400 --> 00:54:33,920
So it follows our instructions more faithfully.

593
00:54:34,000 --> 00:54:35,040
And this is obviously critically important.

594
00:54:35,040 --> 00:54:36,320
Like, don't make something up.

595
00:54:36,800 --> 00:54:39,120
if you don't have a source, like don't hallucinate.

596
00:54:40,160 --> 00:54:45,280
So, so we always want our Madison AI users to be able to take advantage of the most cutting edge models.

597
00:54:45,360 --> 00:54:47,120
And that way, they don't have to, it's not a tradeoff.

598
00:54:47,200 --> 00:54:53,920
Like if they want the smartest models, they don't have to, they don't have to abandon Madison AI and go to like ChatGBT because those models tend to be used under the hood.

599
00:54:54,080 --> 00:55:00,320
We just have a whole bunch of knowledge and orchestration on top of that to make the model even better at being faithful, right?

600
00:55:00,640 --> 00:55:02,320
So, anyway, this is, this is, this is a long way to stay.

601
00:55:02,400 --> 00:55:24,320
It's, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's a, it's, um, and, and, and, but I think it's, essential that whenever we're using, when the church is building Catholic AI, that we can't, we don't want to have like a ghettoized approach where we feel that in order to have a Catholic AI, it means that we have to do it from scratch ourselves, right?

602
00:55:24,560 --> 00:55:27,760
Because essentially what it means is we won't have as good AI.

603
00:55:28,480 --> 00:55:31,760
And then users will feel compelled to go use secular AIs.

604
00:55:31,920 --> 00:55:37,600
I mean, so this is not to say that the project of training a Catholic language model is not worth it.

605
00:55:37,680 --> 00:55:38,800
I mean, it is.

606
00:55:39,520 --> 00:55:42,960
We have effectively trained the world's first Catholic language model called Ephraim.

607
00:55:43,760 --> 00:55:45,680
We're now on version two of Ephraim.

608
00:55:45,920 --> 00:55:47,680
And that project was enormously helpful to us.

609
00:55:48,320 --> 00:55:50,080
And I think it held a lot of promise.

610
00:55:50,480 --> 00:55:54,240
And it could be in, you know, six to eight months, we will release that model to people to test.

611
00:55:54,480 --> 00:55:56,080
And I think it'll be, I think it'll be impressive.

612
00:55:57,280 --> 00:56:02,160
So I think that, you know, trying to train a Catholic model is a worthy endeavor.

613
00:56:02,720 --> 00:56:08,480
But for the foreseeable future, given the pace of AI, it just makes sense to try and be AI complete, right?

614
00:56:08,720 --> 00:56:17,040
Which is, which effectively a way of saying, building a system which allows you to leverage frontier state of the art capabilities within your system.

615
00:56:17,200 --> 00:56:19,920
And so your AI is as intelligent as possible can be.

616
00:56:20,160 --> 00:56:28,560
And Matthew, what is the hardest part of, you know, working with on this problem of building and scaling Catholic AI, would?

617
00:56:30,160 --> 00:56:36,560
Well, I think the hardest part is just, given the fact that, you know, this is about people's souls, right?

618
00:56:37,360 --> 00:56:45,280
And so that's, it's a, it's a terrible responsibility, one that, um, again, like I, I never, like, truly appreciated when we started this project.

619
00:56:45,520 --> 00:56:47,120
It was more of a kind of curiosity to us.

620
00:56:47,280 --> 00:56:51,280
I, I, I never, I never thought we'd be going this deep into it, would scale so quickly.

621
00:56:52,160 --> 00:57:04,800
So we take that responsibility very seriously, which is why we really value the relationships we have, um, with the church, you know, more formally., you know, as I mentioned, we have a scholarly advisory board which has been enormously helpful.

622
00:57:05,040 --> 00:57:13,280
We've had we've worked in collaborations, you know, basically since the very beginning with Pontifical universities, notably the Pontifical Oriental Institute and now the Pontifical Gregory University in this project.

623
00:57:13,440 --> 00:57:17,280
And again, without their help, I don't think I would have had the confidence to proceed in this.

624
00:57:17,520 --> 00:57:39,920
So I would say that, I think, generally, is the hardest thing is just, um, ensuring that we're always, especially when we're, when we're testing a new model that we want to use, making sure we're effectively evaluating that model faithfully, do we understand the way the models are weak and the way the models are strong, and then really making sure that we can mitigate the weaknesses before deploying the model to ensure we don't lead anyone astray.

625
00:57:40,480 --> 00:57:43,760
So that's that's one of the the hardest problems.

626
00:57:44,640 --> 00:57:54,960
The other, the other, I think the hard problem is just recognizing that, you know, there are AIs out there which are doing are very useful and are great and people appreciate it.

627
00:57:54,960 --> 00:57:57,520
And there are AIs out there that are not great and are leading people astray.

628
00:57:57,600 --> 00:58:03,280
I mean, there are AIs which are helping facilitate human flourishing and there are AIs which are actively, I think, in some ways impeding, you know, flourishing.

629
00:58:03,440 --> 00:58:15,600
I mean, one example, not to pick on them because it's not entirely fair, but like character AI, we've heard like we've all probably heard stories of people that, you know, they end up they're becoming their friends and they fall in love with their character, a character AI, and that's obviously not good.

630
00:58:17,040 --> 00:58:20,960
And so those AIs tend to give a kind of a bad name to the technology in general.

631
00:58:21,200 --> 00:58:23,760
And so that becomes something that we have to respond to.

632
00:58:25,440 --> 00:58:44,160
So I think one of the challenges is just effectively educating people about what is the technology, what are its strengths, what are its weaknesses, what are the risks, what are the possible rewards, and just ensuring people understand it so that they leverage the tool effectively and in a responsible way as possible.

633
00:58:44,720 --> 00:58:47,280
And Matthew, I know you've been traveling a lot.

634
00:58:47,360 --> 00:58:52,880
You've been visiting different bishops' conferences, speaking with the laity, with the clergy.

635
00:58:53,840 --> 00:59:00,160
What have you learned when speaking to them about their concerns or where they're at in terms of adoption?

636
00:59:00,480 --> 00:59:06,320
And also, what are the things that you've brought back in terms of informing the work, the research that the team is doing?

637
00:59:06,960 --> 00:59:21,360
Well, I mean, one thing that's given me a lot of hope is initially, and I think I've said this before, but when I initially went to visit the bishops' conferences to talk about kind of a, basically the trajectory of AI, but specifically what we're doing in Catholic AI, I was nervous.

638
00:59:21,600 --> 00:59:24,480
Like I kind of felt like the technology was very new and there was a lot of.

639
00:59:25,120 --> 00:59:28,320
controversy around it, and I wasn't sure how it would be regarded.

640
00:59:28,960 --> 00:59:33,920
But for the most part, I've been very pleasantly surprised.

641
00:59:34,080 --> 00:59:39,840
I think it's just, it's really, for the most part, I think that there's a recognition now amongst the bishops that this technology is not going anywhere.

642
00:59:40,800 --> 00:59:44,960
And it is a very powerful tool, and it's too powerful for the church to ignore, kind of like the printing press, right?

643
00:59:45,200 --> 00:59:46,080
I mean, it would be absurd.

644
00:59:46,160 --> 00:59:47,840
I mean, think of how far we've come with the printing press.

645
00:59:48,000 --> 00:59:50,960
Not to say that it wasn't disruptive and caused some issues as well, but certainly it's.

646
00:59:51,440 --> 00:59:54,160
We're all glad that the church embraced the printing press.

647
00:59:54,400 --> 00:59:56,960
And I think in the same way, we're kind of at the same point.

648
00:59:57,520 --> 00:59:59,840
It's AI can be used for good, it can be used for bad.

649
01:00:00,240 --> 01:00:05,040
But it's still primarily a tool, maybe not a neutral tool, as long as it's relying heavily on human data.

650
01:00:05,200 --> 01:00:16,320
And Pope Francis is aligned on this, but it is still a tool, a tool that, if wielded effectively, can be incredible for advancing things like evangelization.

651
01:00:16,640 --> 01:00:25,280
So I think right now there's just a lot of confusion about what the technology is, how it's going to affect, you know, society, and what is the role for the church in effectively leveraging this technology.

652
01:00:25,360 --> 01:00:28,880
And I found for most of us it's just been, especially amongst the bishops, it's just been curiosity.

653
01:00:29,280 --> 01:00:33,520
They want to better understand the technology so that they can, you know, obviously pastorally respond.

654
01:00:33,680 --> 01:00:37,280
And I think that's kind of where we're at now is the recognition that we have to use the technology.

655
01:00:37,600 --> 01:00:43,120
Now, how do we provide the right pastoral direction and instruction to our priests and to the lady.

656
01:00:44,400 --> 01:01:02,880
And Matthew, of course, the leader of the Catholic Church, Pope Leo XIV, famously took, he's referenced this, he's took his name in honor and in reflection of the work of Pope Leo XIII, who of course shepherded the church, shepherded the faithful through another transition, a revolution, the industrial one.

657
01:01:03,840 --> 01:01:05,600
We're obviously in an AI revolution.

658
01:01:07,680 --> 01:01:09,520
What are your hopes for his papacy?

659
01:01:09,760 --> 01:01:19,200
And also, if you had the opportunity to advise or speak with him, where would you want him to turn his attention in terms of shepherding the people of God.

660
01:01:19,440 --> 01:01:22,480
Well, thank God he's got a lot smarter people advising him than me.

661
01:01:22,560 --> 01:01:27,120
So I expect he's getting very good advice and therefore won't have to rely on mine.

662
01:01:28,160 --> 01:01:31,920
But naturally, I do have some feelings on this.

663
01:01:32,080 --> 01:01:34,240
I mean, and I think I've talked about it a little bit.

664
01:01:34,240 --> 01:01:43,920
I've kind of hinted at it earlier, perhaps, but I think there'll be some people around him who will want the Pope to use his office to try and encourage companies to slow.

665
01:01:45,120 --> 01:01:54,560
companies and countries to slow down the technology to give people time to adapt and to ensure that we have effective regulation in place and that we can come up with the right ethical frameworks.

666
01:01:55,600 --> 01:01:57,520
And I certainly would understand why they did.

667
01:01:57,760 --> 01:01:58,400
they advise that.

668
01:01:58,720 --> 01:02:02,880
But my insight would be we tried that and it failed.

669
01:02:03,200 --> 01:02:05,840
And I don't think the Pope is going to make much of a difference here.

670
01:02:06,080 --> 01:02:12,880
And like I said, in large part because of this kind of China, US, this Cold War that's here.

671
01:02:13,840 --> 01:02:15,840
Neither side feels they can effectively afford to lose.

672
01:02:16,160 --> 01:02:19,200
So I think investing a lot of time and energy in that is not very helpful.

673
01:02:19,520 --> 01:02:27,040
So that being said, I think it's very important for the church to have a official position on how the technology is used in innumerable domains.

674
01:02:27,520 --> 01:02:35,040
And to be very clear that the technology being developed should always be focused on accelerating human flourishing, not impeding it.

675
01:02:36,480 --> 01:02:42,480
And so that doesn't necessarily mean that the technology being further developed means that nobody can lose their jobs, right?

676
01:02:42,640 --> 01:02:55,360
But it's actually, it means that if people are going to have to transition out of their jobs to make room for this technology, those people should be taken care of and have a place to go and they should feel that there's still work for them to do and that they matter.

677
01:02:55,920 --> 01:02:57,040
So I think that's very, very important.

678
01:02:57,200 --> 01:03:01,840
And I think that obviously should be a very important focus of the Pope.

679
01:03:02,000 --> 01:03:02,960
And of course it will be.

680
01:03:04,320 --> 01:03:09,760
But the area that I think is, like I said before, is most important for the Pope, I think, to focus on.

681
01:03:09,840 --> 01:03:11,520
And I think we're very fortunate.

682
01:03:11,760 --> 01:03:20,880
I mean, I think it's prov just helping humanity to get clear about what this whole civilization project is supposed to be about.

683
01:03:21,280 --> 01:03:25,040
Like, how do we build the city of God, right, on Earth?

684
01:03:25,440 --> 01:03:28,960
And what should the city of God look like, feel like?

685
01:03:29,600 --> 01:03:34,480
What would it be like to experience a day in the life of people living in this in the city of God?

686
01:03:35,120 --> 01:03:37,440
It's really important that we get clear about that.

687
01:03:38,400 --> 01:03:40,080
And I can't express that enough.

688
01:03:40,640 --> 01:03:43,440
You know, I think in the past, there have been shows.

689
01:03:44,080 --> 01:03:44,960
I know the show is not perfect.

690
01:03:45,440 --> 01:03:47,200
You know, Star Trek Next Generation is a good example, right?

691
01:03:47,360 --> 01:03:50,160
Where it showed a vision of humanity where we'd beaten poverty, right?

692
01:03:50,480 --> 01:03:52,320
We'd overcome racism and greed.

693
01:03:52,720 --> 01:03:55,360
And humanity was now focused on explororing the stars.

694
01:03:55,600 --> 01:03:59,200
And apparently, as they say in the show, Earth was kind of like paradise, right?

695
01:03:59,760 --> 01:04:01,440
And so, and that was very hopeful.

696
01:04:01,600 --> 01:04:03,200
It's a hopeful sign that we can do this, right?

697
01:04:03,440 --> 01:04:05,200
And so it showed some kind of vision of the future.

698
01:04:05,520 --> 01:04:09,440
And then in principle, I know that inspired people like Trekkies, right, to say, like, that's it.

699
01:04:09,680 --> 01:04:10,720
I want to be part of that human project.

700
01:04:10,960 --> 01:04:15,840
And so I'm going to dedicate my life to trying to emulate the values that they live by, and I want to try and build that kind of world.

701
01:04:16,080 --> 01:04:18,880
Well, I kind of think we need that now.

702
01:04:19,200 --> 01:04:22,640
Like we need to have that kind of, we need to have that kind of concrete vision of the future.

703
01:04:23,440 --> 01:04:35,280
And, and for no other reason, um, I I I would say we won't be able effectively to regulate the technology unless we have a, um, a clear sense of how we want the technology to be developed and what problems we should be pointing it at.

704
01:04:35,360 --> 01:04:41,040
I mean, like when you're regulating, you're saying, you know, the technology should be used in this way but shouldn't be used in that way, you know, effectively.

705
01:04:41,280 --> 01:04:43,360
Well, then how do we make that prudential determination?

706
01:04:43,520 --> 01:04:46,960
That's based on some understanding of what the human person is about, what civilization is about, right?

707
01:04:47,200 --> 01:04:57,120
And so we have to have some sense of ideally what what people should be doing with their time and energies and how civilization is, should be built in order to help facilitate, you know, human flourishing.

708
01:04:57,440 --> 01:04:59,040
And I think we're not clear about that at all.

709
01:04:59,760 --> 01:05:09,040
So I think there's a there's a I think there's a real chance of a bunch of different interests getting involved, you know, have very specific ideas about how the technology should be developed.

710
01:05:09,200 --> 01:05:12,560
And I'm not saying that those ideas are always going to be bad, but I they may not always be consistent.

711
01:05:12,960 --> 01:05:24,160
And so I think that the Pope just needs to remind us of what human life is supposed to be about, what civilization is supposed to be all about, and what we, as I said before, should be paddling towards.

712
01:05:24,560 --> 01:05:30,960
And I think if he can effectively do that, that will be enormously beneficial, not just for the church, but actually for the AI industry itself.

713
01:05:31,040 --> 01:05:36,240
I mean, even the heads of the AI labs, I mean, this is something that they're concerned they have kids, like many of those founders.

714
01:05:36,320 --> 01:05:48,080
So they're worried about the future and they want to make sure that technology is directed to the right places and they want effective regulation, make sure that it's developed well and to make sure that the transition to the new AI and robotics age is done peacefully and doesn't leave people behind.

715
01:05:48,720 --> 01:05:49,680
But that requires vision.

716
01:05:50,160 --> 01:05:54,160
And I think that's one thing that we seriously lack nowadays.

717
01:05:54,880 --> 01:05:59,760
